# A TEMPLATE. Copy it, don't edit it:  cp .env.local.example .env.local
#
# This file is committed to a public repository. Nothing real goes in it — a
# value pasted here is published the moment it is pushed, and stays in the
# history even after it is removed. Put real values in .env.local (gitignored),
# or in Vercel → Settings → Environment Variables, which overrides both.

# ── Supabase ── app.supabase.com → Project Settings → API ────────────────────

# Public by design: both are compiled into the browser bundle. RLS is what
# protects the data, not the secrecy of this key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=your_supabase_project_url_here
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your_supabase_anon_key_here

# Required for building, and never NEXT_PUBLIC_: it bypasses RLS entirely.
# The build endpoint is called by n8n with no user session behind it, so it
# writes the generated page under this key. Nothing else uses it.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your_service_role_key_here

# ── Build orchestrator ── the n8n workflow behind the chat, see n8n/README.md ─

# The published workflow's production webhook (/webhook/, not /webhook-test/).
# Without it the chat answers "Building is not connected yet".
# N8N_WEBHOOK_URL=https://<your-instance>.app.n8n.cloud/webhook/api/v1/build

# Sent as the X-QuickStark-Token header, and checked by the Header Auth
# credential on the Webhook node — the same string has to be in both. Not
# optional: the workflow writes to `projects` with the service_role key, so an
# unauthenticated webhook lets anyone who learns the URL overwrite any row.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# N8N_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=

# ── Image sources ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS SECTION IS OPTIONAL. An image provider is a plug-in
# source, never a core dependency: with all of them unset the builder still
# classifies, plans, generates and stores a complete project. Nothing errors,
# nothing is logged at a user, and nobody is asked to configure an API.
#
# The chain is tried in order and the first source that answers wins:
#   project assets → curated library → Unsplash → Pexels → AI generation
#
# 1. The QuickStark curated library — the source that needs no third party.
#    The catalogue is in src/lib/builder/assets/providers/curated.ts; this is
#    where its files are served from. Unset, the library reports itself
#    misconfigured and is skipped rather than serving addresses that 404.
# CURATED_ASSETS_BASE_URL=https://<project>.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/quickstark-library
# CURATED_LIBRARY_ENABLED=false     # switch it off without unsetting the URL

# 2. Stock libraries. Optional enhancements over the curated library.
#    Unsplash requires attribution; the adapter honours it and registers the
#    download their guidelines ask for.
# UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=
# PEXELS_API_KEY=
# UNSPLASH_ENABLED=false
# PEXELS_ENABLED=false

# 3. Generated imagery. Off unless explicitly enabled, because it is the only
#    source that costs money per picture. No vendor is wired in yet — see
#    src/lib/builder/assets/providers/ai.ts.
# AI_IMAGE_ENABLED=true
# AI_IMAGE_ENDPOINT=
# AI_IMAGE_API_KEY=

# Reorder or narrow the chain without touching code. Anything left out is not
# consulted at all.
# ASSET_PROVIDER_ORDER=project,curated,unsplash,pexels,ai

# There is no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY here. Pages are generated in the orchestrator,
# under n8n's own Anthropic credential — the app never calls a model.

# ── Crypto checkout ── the wallets money is collected in, see docs/PAYMENTS.md ─

# A coin is offered ONLY when its address is set. There is no default and no
# fallback, which is why an unconfigured deployment says "Crypto payments are
# not switched on" rather than quietly listing coins nobody can be paid in.
#
# These are receiving addresses: public by nature, and safe to keep in Vercel's
# environment. They are still not committed here, because the address in this
# file is the address a real payment would be sent to, and a placeholder that
# reached production would send somebody's money nowhere.
# CRYPTO_WALLET_BTC=
# CRYPTO_WALLET_ETH=
# CRYPTO_WALLET_LTC=
# CRYPTO_WALLET_XRP=
# The tag the XRP payment must carry to be credited. Set it whenever the XRP
# address above is on an exchange or any shared wallet.
# CRYPTO_WALLET_XRP_TAG=

# Optional. A Lightning address or LNURL; set one and the Lightning toggle
# appears on that coin. Leave unset and it is never offered.
# CRYPTO_LIGHTNING_BTC=

# ── BTCPay Server ── the thing that watches the chain ───────────────────────
#
# Without these, a payment arrives and sits there until somebody runs
# `npm run settle`. With them, BTCPay issues one address per invoice, notices
# when it is paid, and credits the account within a confirmation.
#
# It never holds your money: the store is configured watch-only from an account
# xpub/zpub, so funds are paid straight into your own wallet.
#
# All four are needed. The first three issue invoices; the fourth verifies the
# callbacks, and without it nothing settles automatically no matter what the
# other three say.
# BTCPAY_URL=https://your-instance.example.com
# BTCPAY_STORE_ID=
# BTCPAY_API_KEY=
# Store -> Webhooks -> the secret shown when you create the webhook. Point the
# webhook at https://<your-app>/api/payments/crypto/btcpay and subscribe it to
# "An invoice has been settled".
# BTCPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# The secret every settlement callback is signed with. Without it, payments can
# be made and never credited — /api/payments/crypto/webhook refuses every call.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# CRYPTO_PAYMENTS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Optional. Prices come from CoinGecko's public API; a key raises the rate
# limit, and CRYPTO_RATE_<TICKER> pins one coin's rate for a deployment with no
# outbound network (staging, or a business pricing from its own desk).
# COINGECKO_API_KEY=
# CRYPTO_RATE_BTC=

# ── Settlement that does not depend on being told ─────────────────────────
# The sweep at /api/cron/reconcile reads the open orders, reads the Bitcoin
# chain directly, and settles the ones that were paid. It is what credits a
# customer when BTCPay is down, gone, or was never told to call back — nothing
# above has to be working for it to run.
#
# Required, or the endpoint refuses every caller. Any long random string:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Vercel's scheduler sends it as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`
# automatically; anything else scheduling the sweep sends the same header.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Optional, and only for the orders the sweep cannot settle on its own — coin on
# an address that does not cover its order, which is a judgement rather than a
# calculation. With no key set they are reported in the sweep's own response and
# in the log instead; detection never depends on delivery.
# Free tier at resend.com. ALERT_FROM needs a domain verified with Resend; left
# unset it sends from onboarding@resend.dev, which always works.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# ALERT_EMAIL=
# ALERT_FROM=

# Verify the whole chain with:  npm run check:builder
# Both halves of the checkout are reported by /api/health as
# cryptoCheckoutConfigured and cryptoSettlementConfigured, and whether the
# BTCPay instance those variables name is actually alive as btcpayReachable.

# ── Model providers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One per maker the composer's picker offers. A model whose provider has no key
# here is refused by /api/build with a sentence naming the maker, rather than
# offered and then failing mid-build.
#
# These are read on the server only. The keys that actually SEND the request
# live in n8n's credential store — see n8n/README.md. What these decide is
# whether a model is offered at all.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# From Google AI Studio. The free tier is permanent and rate-limited rather
# than a credit balance: roughly 10-15 requests a minute on the Flash models,
# and Pro is not in it.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# ── Pausing the builder ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to "true" when generation cannot work — a provider outage, a key with no
# balance. Builds are then refused before anything is classified, charged or
# written, and the composer says so instead of showing a spinner that cannot
# finish. Handing over a file and Undo still work: neither needs a model.
#
# Unset, empty, or anything unrecognised means RUNNING. A typo must not take
# the app down.
BUILDER_PAUSED=
# Optional. Overrides the wording for this particular outage. Keep it to a
# sentence or two, say whose fault it is, and say whether anything was charged
# — that is the first thing anyone wonders.
BUILDER_PAUSED_MESSAGE=
